# copal-alpine-linux tracks NO binaries. This is the opposite of the policy in
# the original arm-pi-zero repository, which vendored the Alpine payload and the
# Mini vMac working set on purpose. That repository could never be published:
# minivmac/macOS755.dsk.zip was 894 MB, past GitHub's 100 MB per-file limit, and
# the Macintosh Plus ROM beside it is copyrighted Apple material.
#
# Nothing is lost. copal-prep.sh downloads the Alpine payload and verifies its
# published SHA256; fetch-minivmac.sh assembles the Mini vMac set on demand. The
# binaries were always a convenience, never a dependency.

# Everything generated. One line, because everything generated lands under
# build/ -- images, EFI variable stores, transcripts, and build/cache/ holding
# the checksum-verified Alpine payloads. The two have different lifetimes and
# make treats them differently (`make clean` empties build/ but steps around
# the cache; `make distclean` takes both), but neither is ever committed.
build/

# Where the cache lived before it moved under build/. Kept ignored so an older
# working copy cannot stage 1.8 GB of payloads by accident.
work/
cache/

# Disk images, firmware, and anything a target boots from
*.img
*.qcow2
*.raw
*.dmg
*.iso
*.fd
*.utm/

# Payloads and packages
*.tar.gz
*.tgz
*.zip
*.apk

# Emulator media -- ROMs and disk images are not ours to redistribute
*.ROM
*.rom
*.dsk
*.smi.bin

# Logs and transcripts
*.log
run-log-*.txt

# macOS metadata
.DS_Store
._*
.Spotlight-V100
.fseventsd
.Trashes

# Generated per-machine configuration. None of this is ever supposed to appear
# in the repository, and it does not today -- copal-prep.sh writes these onto
# the card or image, which is itself ignored above. They are listed anyway
# because the cost of being wrong is asymmetric: they carry the git identity
# read from THIS Mac's git config, the chosen hostname and the admin username.
#
# The identity is not a credential and nothing authenticates with it -- but it
# is a real name and a real address belonging to whoever wrote the card, and
# that does not belong in a public repository just because it was convenient to
# put on a boot partition.
#
# answers.txt is the exception and the reason to be careful: since `make
# answers` it also carries COPAL_ROOT_PW_HASH, a SHA-512 crypt hash of the root
# password. A hash is not a password and the file is written 0600, but it is
# offline-crackable at whatever rate the password deserves, so it is a
# credential in every way that matters. It has been ignored all along -- listed
# here since before it held anything sensitive -- which is the only reason
# adding a hash to it was safe to do.
copal.conf
copal-git
answers.txt
usercfg.txt
authorized_keys
firstrun.log
copal-auto
copal-timings

# The build transcripts. *.log above already covers these; named because they
# quote the identity verbatim in the "Git identity offered:" line.
copal-prep-auto*.log
copal-vm-check.log
run-log-*.txt
